1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,368
Position tells us where the particle is.

2
00:00:02,638 --> 00:00:05,320
Velocity is the derivative of position.

3
00:00:05,590 --> 00:00:08,365
Acceleration is the derivative of velocity.

4
00:00:08,635 --> 00:00:11,665
Speed is the absolute value of velocity.

5
00:00:12,785 --> 00:00:15,572
Differentiate position and factor the result.

6
00:00:15,842 --> 00:00:21,740
The particle is at rest when velocity is zero, so the rest times are one second and two seconds.

7
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:24,578
Differentiate velocity.

8
00:00:24,848 --> 00:00:27,948
Acceleration is zero at one point five seconds.

9
00:00:28,218 --> 00:00:34,058
That does not mean the particle is at rest: its velocity there is negative one point five feet per second.

10
00:00:35,178 --> 00:00:42,612
To determine speeding up, compare the signs of velocity and acceleration on intervals separated by one, one

11
00:00:42,612 --> 00:00:44,001
point five, and two.

12
00:00:44,271 --> 00:00:47,185
Do not decide from the sign of acceleration alone.

13
00:00:48,305 --> 00:00:52,253
Velocity has signs positive, negative, negative, positive.

14
00:00:52,523 --> 00:00:56,760
Acceleration has signs negative, negative, positive, positive.

15
00:00:57,030 --> 00:01:01,024
Matching signs increase speed; opposite signs decrease speed.

16
00:01:02,144 --> 00:01:07,241
The particle speeds up between one and one point five seconds, and after two seconds.

17
00:01:07,511 --> 00:01:12,410
It slows down between zero and one, and between one point five and two.

18
00:01:12,680 --> 00:01:16,674
These are open intervals; transition instants are handled separately.

19
00:01:17,794 --> 00:01:22,113
Problem forty one asks the same five motion questions as problem forty.

20
00:01:22,383 --> 00:01:25,425
Start with velocity, the derivative of position.

21
00:01:26,545 --> 00:01:29,029
Factor the velocity to find the rest times.

22
00:01:29,299 --> 00:01:32,539
Velocity is zero at one half second and one second.

23
00:01:32,809 --> 00:01:34,910
Its units are feet per second.

24
00:01:36,030 --> 00:01:40,326
Differentiate velocity to find acceleration in feet per second squared.

25
00:01:40,596 --> 00:01:43,254
It changes sign at three quarters of a second.

26
00:01:44,374 --> 00:01:48,577
Partition the allowed time line at one half, three quarters, and one.

27
00:01:48,847 --> 00:01:52,737
Test the signs of velocity and acceleration in each open interval.

28
00:01:53,857 --> 00:01:58,489
As in problem forty, the signs are opposite, matching, opposite, matching.

29
00:01:58,759 --> 00:02:04,541
A negative velocity with a negative acceleration means the particle is speeding up in the negative direction.

30
00:02:05,661 --> 00:02:10,874
The particle slows down before one half second and between three quarters and one second.

31
00:02:11,144 --> 00:02:15,416
It speeds up between one half and three quarters, and after one second.

32
00:02:15,686 --> 00:02:18,345
This completes all five requested parts.

33
00:02:19,465 --> 00:02:22,263
The cylinder radius is fixed at five meters.

34
00:02:22,533 --> 00:02:27,026
This is why we can replace pi r squared with the constant twenty five pi.

35
00:02:28,146 --> 00:02:30,386
Differentiate with respect to time.

36
00:02:30,656 --> 00:02:32,131
Only h changes.

37
00:02:32,401 --> 00:02:36,267
There is no radius-rate term because the radius is constant.

38
00:02:37,387 --> 00:02:40,499
Substitute the incoming volume rate and divide.

39
00:02:40,769 --> 00:02:45,111
The water height rises at about zero point zero three eight two meters per minute.

40
00:02:46,231 --> 00:02:47,914
Let a be the changing edge length.

41
00:02:48,184 --> 00:02:53,362
Both volume and surface area depend on a, so use a as the bridge between their rates.

42
00:02:54,482 --> 00:02:58,186
Differentiate the volume and use edge length thirty centimeters.

43
00:02:58,456 --> 00:03:02,287
This gives the edge-length rate, not the requested area rate yet.

44
00:03:03,407 --> 00:03:07,215
Differentiate surface area and substitute the edge-length rate.

45
00:03:07,485 --> 00:03:10,806
The final answer is four thirds square centimeters per minute.

46
00:03:11,926 --> 00:03:13,957
Measure t in hours after noon.

47
00:03:14,227 --> 00:03:18,546
Let x be the east west separation and z the north south separation.

48
00:03:18,816 --> 00:03:25,840
Before the ships pass east west, x decreases at thirty five kilometers per hour, while z increases at twenty

49
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:26,165
five.

50
00:03:27,285 --> 00:03:30,350
The distance is the hypotenuse of a right triangle.

51
00:03:30,620 --> 00:03:36,089
Differentiate the squared distance equation with respect to time before substituting the time.

52
00:03:36,359 --> 00:03:38,994
Every changing quantity contributes its rate.

53
00:03:40,114 --> 00:03:42,726
At four p m, four hours have passed.

54
00:03:42,996 --> 00:03:48,140
The horizontal separation is ten kilometers, and the vertical separation is one hundred.

55
00:03:48,410 --> 00:03:51,568
Use these current values, not the noon separation.

56
00:03:52,688 --> 00:03:56,205
Divide by D and substitute both rates with their signs.

57
00:03:56,475 --> 00:04:02,774
The result is positive, about twenty one point three nine kilometers per hour, so the ships are moving farther

58
00:04:02,774 --> 00:04:04,033
apart at that instant.

59
00:04:05,153 --> 00:04:08,753
The cone has total radius two meters and height four meters.

60
00:04:09,023 --> 00:04:11,797
Water enters at two cubic meters per minute.

61
00:04:12,067 --> 00:04:16,468
Unlike a cylinder, the water surface radius changes as the water rises.

62
00:04:17,588 --> 00:04:21,976
Use similar triangles to replace the changing radius with h over two.

63
00:04:22,246 --> 00:04:26,449
Substitute this relationship into the volume formula before differentiating.

64
00:04:26,719 --> 00:04:29,970
Now volume depends on just one unknown changing length.

65
00:04:31,090 --> 00:04:33,354
Differentiate with respect to time.

66
00:04:33,624 --> 00:04:38,395
The derivative of h cubed is three h squared times d h over d t.

67
00:04:38,665 --> 00:04:41,336
The factor of three reduces twelve to four.

68
00:04:42,456 --> 00:04:45,846
Now substitute a water depth of three meters and solve.

69
00:04:46,116 --> 00:04:49,877
The height rises at about zero point two eight three meters per minute.

70
00:04:50,147 --> 00:04:55,035
Check the units: a height rate uses meters per minute, not cubic meters per minute.

71
00:04:56,155 --> 00:04:59,777
Let x be distance along the path from the point nearest the light.

72
00:05:00,047 --> 00:05:03,078
Let theta be the angle from the perpendicular to the path.

73
00:05:03,348 --> 00:05:08,503
The perpendicular distance is twenty feet, so tangent theta equals x over twenty.

74
00:05:08,773 --> 00:05:11,582
We take x increasing as the man walks away.

75
00:05:12,702 --> 00:05:15,013
Differentiate with respect to time.

76
00:05:15,283 --> 00:05:20,484
The angle changes, so the derivative of tangent includes d theta over d t.

77
00:05:21,604 --> 00:05:25,365
At the requested position, tangent theta is three quarters.

78
00:05:25,635 --> 00:05:32,474
The identity secant squared equals one plus tangent squared avoids needing to calculate the angle itself.

79
00:05:33,594 --> 00:05:38,400
Divide by secant squared to obtain zero point one two eight radians per second.

80
00:05:38,670 --> 00:05:40,888
This is the rotation-rate magnitude.

81
00:05:41,158 --> 00:05:46,289
If the man were approaching the nearest point, the signed angle rate would be negative instead.

82
00:05:47,409 --> 00:05:49,174
The building height is constant.

83
00:05:49,444 --> 00:05:54,402
The sun angle is decreasing, so its signed rate is negative one quarter radian per hour.

84
00:05:54,672 --> 00:05:56,285
Let L be the shadow length.

85
00:05:57,405 --> 00:06:01,480
Use the right triangle: opposite over adjacent equals tangent.

86
00:06:01,750 --> 00:06:05,315
Solving for shadow length gives four hundred cotangent theta.

87
00:06:06,435 --> 00:06:08,826
Differentiate the cotangent expression.

88
00:06:09,096 --> 00:06:14,797
Its negative derivative multiplies the negative angle rate, so the shadow length increases.

89
00:06:15,917 --> 00:06:20,387
At pi over six, sine is one half, so cosecant squared is four.

90
00:06:20,657 --> 00:06:23,954
The shadow length is increasing at four hundred feet per hour.
